# Runic Tomes Lexicon
# The oldest attested runic system (Elder Futhark) is layer 0. Later systems ride on top
# and inherit from their parent when a letter or rune is not defined in the layer itself.
#
# Every rune row is checked in CI: glyph == code point, uname == Unicode 17.0 UnicodeData,
# and `reversible` must match what the transliteration rules actually do.
# Code points are written U+XXXX on purpose: TOON reads bare 16E0 as a number.
# Research and sources: docs/research/runes.md
#
# Latin -> runes is a MODERN CONVENTION (no scholarly standard exists for that direction).
# Runes -> Latin uses the conventional runological transliteration of each rune.
meta:
  name: runic-tomes-lexicon
  version: 1.0.0
  unicode: "17.0"
  license: MIT
  wordSeparator: ᛫
# parent = historical lineage. rulesFrom = which set fills in runes and Latin rules this set
# does not define itself (Younger Futhark has none, so Elder runes never leak into it).
sets[8|]{id|name|layer|parent|rulesFrom|from|to|historical|note|source}:
  elder|Elder Futhark|0|""|""|150|800|true|"Oldest securely read inscription: Vimose comb (harja, c. 160 CE). Kylver stone (c. 400) is the first full row. Most inscriptions use no word dividers."|"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elder_Futhark"
  futhorc|Anglo-Frisian Futhorc|1|elder|elder|450|1100|true|Grows from 28 to about 33 runes; Northumbrian additions and manuscript-only cealc.|"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anglo-Saxon_runes"
  younger-long|Younger Futhark (long-branch)|1|elder|""|800|1100|true|Reduced to 16 runes by the late 8th century; mostly carved on stone.|"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Younger_Futhark"
  younger-short|Younger Futhark (short-twig)|2|younger-long|younger-long|800|1100|true|Also called Rök runes; mostly carved on wood. Staveless (Hälsinge) runes are allographs of these and have no code points.|"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Younger_Futhark"
  medieval|Medieval (dotted) runes|2|younger-long|younger-long|1100|1500|true|Dots distinguish sound pairs; dotting was optional and spelling varied.|"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medieval_runes"
  franks-casket|Franks Casket cryptic runes|3|futhorc|futhorc|700|750|true|Cryptic vowel forms from one early 8th-century whalebone casket; Futhorc otherwise.|"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franks_Casket"
  golden-numbers|Golden number runes|3|medieval|medieval|""|""|true|Calendar symbols for golden numbers 17 to 19 only. Runes had no digit system; digits are never converted.|"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/U16A0.pdf"
  tolkien|Tolkienian runes|3|futhorc|futhorc|1937|""|false|Modern and fictional. Based on Anglo-Saxon runes. Full Cirth is Private Use Area only and deliberately excluded.|"https://www.unicode.org/L2/L2011/11096r-n4013r-runic-additions.pdf"
